---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import { projects } from '../data/projects';
---

<Layout>
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5">
		{/* Hero. The masthead above already carries the name at full size, so this
		    says what the name does and hands off to About — the page's only route
		    there since the nav links came out (2026-08-01). Held to --measure so a
		    wide viewport can't run the line out past a readable length.

		    It used to be the about page's opening line verbatim, matched to its
		    type size so arriving from either direction read as one voice. That
		    stopped being true when this line became its own sentence: the two
		    pages now open differently on purpose, the home page saying what the
		    work IS and About saying who does it. The size match went with it —
		    about-page had followed About's 08-02 step down to the base 19px on
		    THIS line too, and that change was dropped when the two branches were
		    merged, since there is no longer a shared sentence to keep in step.
		    If the two are ever written the same again, re-match the size. */}
		{/* Centred, both lines, each on its own axis (2026-08-02, Hiro). This
		    REPLACES the shared-left-edge lockup from earlier the same day, and the
		    reason it can: that arrangement existed because the second line was a
		    bare text link that had to read as hanging off the sentence, which a
		    second centring axis would have broken. It's a button now — a boxed
		    control is self-evidently its own object, so it doesn't need the
		    sentence's left edge to say so, and the hero reads as one deliberate
		    centred block instead. */}
		{/* Padding stepped up one full stop 2026-08-03 (Hiro: let the hero breathe
		    more) — 64/80 → 96/112, and 80/96 → 112/128 from sm up. Stepped again
		    2026-08-05, same ask and the same way: 112/128, and 128/144 from sm up.
		    The 1rem top/bottom ASYMMETRY is deliberate and survives both
		    increases: the space below has to separate the hero from the projects
		    grid, while the space above only has to clear the nav bar, which is
		    already a ruled edge. Keep stepping BOTH values together if it goes
		    up again — the gap is the point, not the individual numbers.

		    ⚠ 2026-08-11 CUT ROUGHLY IN HALF — 112/128 → 56/64, 128/144 → 64/72.
		    This is the three increases above being UNDONE, and the reason is that
		    they were the wrong lever. Read as a sequence they are the tell: a
		    hero that felt thin three separate times, answered three times with
		    air. The section was 426px tall and 103px of it was ink — 76% air
		    holding a sentence set at 1.125× body, which is reading size, not
		    display size. Whitespace was compensating for type that wasn't doing
		    display work. The fix went to SIZE (see the tagline below); the air
		    came off in the same pass because at 43.7px the sentence holds the
		    section on its own and the old padding read as a gap rather than as
		    breathing room.
		    The asymmetry survives, halved with everything else: 0.5rem now
		    rather than 1rem, same direction, same reason.
		    Measured, both themes, 1440×900: the section goes 426px → 311px and
		    its ink 103px → 150px, so the air drops from 76% to 52%. The first
		    project card moves from y=563 to y=448. Note the section did NOT
		    halve — the padding did, and the type took half of it back. That is
		    the trade this change is: the same vertical space, spent on ink. */}
		{/* The hero reveals as ONE block (2026-08-06, Hiro) — the sentence and its
		    button enter together, --i 0 of a two-beat cascade (the projects grid is
		    --i 1). They were two entries (--i 0 and 1, with the cards at +2) until
		    the per-element stagger was flagged against the migaki carve-out —
		    "one entrance on the hero, everything else static" — and collapsed:
		    elements inside a block enter WITH it, same rule the hairline finale
		    already enforces for the card dividers. */}
		<section class="reveal pt-14 pb-16 text-center sm:pt-16 sm:pb-[4.5rem]" style="--i: 0">
			{/* Still capped at --measure so a wide viewport can't run the line out
			    past a readable length; mx-auto centres the box the cap creates. */}
			{/* --ink-hero, a step PAST the body ink away from the paper (2026-08-03,
			    Hiro: this line and the button should contrast more, closer to
			    white). It briefly went the other way, to --ink-soft, on a reading
			    of "lighter" as quieter — wrong, and the correction is worth
			    keeping: in the dark theme this line already sat near the top of
			    the range, so "lighter" could only have meant brighter.
			    This makes the sentence the highest-contrast text on the site,
			    ahead of body ink. That is the point — it is the page's opening
			    statement and the only thing above the rule — but it does mean the
			    hero now out-contrasts the wordmark, so if the mark ever needs to
			    lead again, it is this token it has to answer to. See --ink-hero in
			    global.css for why the two themes are proportional rather than
			    inverse. */}
			{/* ⚠ 2026-08-11 THIS LINE BECAME DISPLAY TYPE — 21.375px → 35.625px,
			    and the padding above came off in the same pass. The two are one
			    change: the hero was 76% air because the sentence was set at
			    1.125× body and couldn't hold the section, so every previous pass
			    added padding instead. Size is the lever that was never pulled.

			    THE CEILING IS THE PROJECT H1's SIZE, EXACTLY, and that is the
			    whole reasoning. It first shipped at 43.7px — the middle of the
			    critique's 40–48px ask — and Hiro's read was "too much". The
			    problem wasn't only the number: at 43.7 this line sat ABOVE
			    everything else on the site, inventing a display tier the scale
			    didn't have, on a site whose headings have been coming DOWN (the
			    about greeting went 4xl → 23.75px on 08-02 for the same reason).
			    At 35.625px it is level with the project h1 instead — the hero
			    takes the site's existing top size rather than a new one above
			    it. So the scale still runs 16.625px prose → 35.625px, and the
			    home page's opening statement now ties the largest heading rather
			    than outranking it. It is still 1.88× body, which is display work;
			    it was 1.125× before, which was not.
			    Read that as the constraint, not the number: if the project h1
			    ever moves, this should move with it.

			    FLUID, not a breakpoint step. It went out as text-[2rem] with an
			    sm:text-[2.3rem] and that was wrong at both ends — 38px held all
			    the way down to 320px, which put FOUR lines on a phone. A clamp
			    scales through the middle instead of stepping once, so the line
			    count changes where the words actually run out rather than where
			    a breakpoint happens to sit. Measured, no horizontal overflow at
			    any width:
			      320px   28.5px (the floor)     4 lines
			      390px   28.5px                 3 lines
			      641px   32.05px                2 lines  ← the br turns on here
			      1440px  35.625px (the ceiling) 2 lines
			    The 5vw middle term puts the ceiling at ~713px, so every desktop
			    width gets the full 35.625px. The floor is the dial for the phone
			    case: 320px takes a fourth line, which is the accepted cost of
			    holding 28.5px at 390px where most phones actually are.

			    Leading 1.15, down from leading-relaxed's 1.625. A prose leading
			    on a two-line display sentence opens a gap the eye reads as two
			    separate statements; at 1.15 the two lines lock into one block.
			    It rode 1.1 at the 43.7px size — leading wants to open slightly
			    as size comes down, so the two moved together.

			    ── The APCA note this replaces, because it is now MOOT ──
			    The 2026-08-04 audit put this line 0.125px under the floor in the
			    LIGHT theme at w300 (Lc 88.5 wants ≥21.5px; it was 21.375px), and
			    that miss was accepted deliberately — Hiro asked for it lighter
			    knowing the cost. The old note recorded that SIZE clears the
			    shortfall as easily as weight. It just did, with room to spare:
			    at 35.625px the line clears the floor at any weight the face
			    offers, so the deliberate miss is retired rather than carried.
			    Note this survives the size coming back down from 43.7px — the
			    floor it missed was 21.5px, so there is a wide band underneath
			    before it becomes a live concern again.

			    Weight rides --weight-ui, NOT a pinned number, and that is the
			    correction worth recording. The critique asked for 450–500 and
			    this shipped as a literal font-[450] first — which quietly broke
			    the rule at the top of global.css: light runs ONE NOTCH HEAVIER
			    than dark for all INK text, through the tokens, so that dark ink
			    on a light ground buys back the stroke it loses. Pinned numbers
			    are reserved for things that aren't ink on paper (the button's
			    label, the wordmark). This line is ink on paper, so a pinned 450
			    would have given light and dark the same weight and undone that
			    compensation on the theme that needs it most.
			    --weight-ui is 400 dark / 500 light, which BRACKETS the 450–500
			    ask rather than splitting it — the theme that draws thinner gets
			    the lighter number, which is the whole point of the asymmetry.
			    If it reads heavy, the next stop down is --weight-body (300/400),
			    not a literal: large type needs less weight than small, and the
			    size is already doing most of the work here. */}
			<p class="mx-auto max-w-[var(--measure)] text-[clamp(1.5rem,5vw,1.875rem)] leading-[1.15] font-[var(--weight-ui)] text-[var(--ink-hero)]">
				{/* The break is EXPLICIT and it has to be. Left to wrap inside
				    --measure's 665px, the sentence splits mid-phrase — it breaks
				    after "good" or "sense", cutting "good sense and taste", which
				    is the half doing the actual work.
				    NO WIDTH CAP CAN FIX THAT, and the reason is a fixed relation
				    between the two clauses rather than a tuning problem: line 2
				    ("with good sense and taste.", 482px at 35.625px) is WIDER
				    than "Design engineering, with" (444px). So any cap roomy
				    enough to hold the second clause on one line is also roomy
				    enough to pull "with" up onto the first. The gap scales with
				    the type, so this stays true at every size — it held at 43.7px
				    too (591 vs 546). Nor does text-wrap: balance help: it targets
				    EVEN lines, which is the same break one word over.
				    So the break goes where the sentence's own break is: the comma.
				    Line 1 short, line 2 long, which is the right way round for a
				    centred pair — the eye lands on the shorter clause first.
				    Hidden below sm, where the clause no longer fits on one line
				    anyway and has to wrap on its own terms — forcing the break
				    there would just add a line to a column already wrapping.
				    sm (640px) is the tightest width where it IS active, so that
				    is the one to check if the copy ever changes: at 641px the
				    second clause measures 434px inside a 583px column, so there
				    is ~149px of slack and a longer sentence overflows there
				    first. */}
				Design engineering,<br class="hidden sm:inline" /> with good sense and taste.
			</p>
			{/* The page's only route to /about, so it's the site's one real button.
			    mt-6 rather than the old link's mt-4 — a control needs more air off
			    the line above it than a word set in the same size did.

			    The arrow is DRAWN AT REST and doesn't animate, unlike the project
			    cards' (which appear on hover). Different job: on a card the arrow
			    is a flourish on something already obviously clickable — bordered
			    image, whole-card cursor, a panel that lights up. Here it's the only
			    thing distinguishing a button that navigates from a button that
			    performs an action, and that's information you want before you
			    hover, not after. .btn's flex + gap owns the spacing, so the glyph
			    needs no margin of its own. */}
			<p class="mt-6">
				<a href="/about" class="btn">
					About Hiro Eernisse
					<svg
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2"
						stroke-linecap="round"
						stroke-linejoin="round"
						aria-hidden="true"
						class="inline-block h-[0.85rem] w-[0.85rem]"
					>
						<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
						<polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
					</svg>
				</a>
			</p>
		</section>

		{/* Projects */}
		{/* The second of the page's two hairlines (2026-08-03, Hiro), the first
		    being the nav's own bottom edge. Drawn in global.css (#projects::before)
		    off the shared --rule gradient, which fades both of them out at their
		    ends — so they read as one device: the page is indexed into a bar, an
		    opening statement and a body of work.

		    This one is held to the CONTENT column rather than run full-bleed like
		    the nav's. Different jobs — the nav's line is the underside of a bar
		    that spans the viewport, while this one is a break inside the column,
		    and it lands on the same left edge as the leftmost card's year, the
		    wordmark above it and the copyright below. A full-bleed rule here would
		    read as a second bar rather than as a division of the text block.

		    The space is deliberately lopsided: the hero keeps its full breathing
		    room above the line, and the list opens at pt-12 below it, so the rule
		    belongs to the section it introduces rather than floating between two
		    equal gaps. */}
		<section id="projects" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-14">
			{/* The grid, the travelling hover panel and the script that drives it
			    all moved into ProjectGrid.astro on 2026-08-10, when the project
			    pages grew a "More projects" strip and needed the same three things.
			    Nothing changed in the move — the rendered markup here is identical
			    — so every comment that used to live at this spot now lives in that
			    component, next to the code it describes.

			    What stays a decision of THIS page: .reveal and --i 1. The grid is
			    the second of the home page's two blocks (the hero is --i 0), and
			    the reveal sits on #project-list rather than on #projects so the
			    section's rule (#projects::before) stays OUTSIDE it — page-level
			    structure enters in the hairline finale, not with the content. See
			    the finale block in global.css, and the Props note in the component
			    for why the caller owns this rather than the grid. */}
			<ProjectGrid projects={projects} class="reveal" style="--i: 1" />
		</section>
	</main>

	{/* text-xs, down from text-sm (2026-08-03, Hiro). A copyright line is the
	    lowest-priority text on the page, and it was sharing a size with real
	    body copy. The projects grid stepped down in the same pass. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
		<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
	</footer>

</Layout>
